r the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y=7,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x)=7.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{3}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot 7 \\\\ {x}^{3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y & =7{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{3})\\,dx & =\\int 7{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\frac{7{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{7x}{4}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186578005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59cfc051-0630-5000-ae0c-1f8137c26a3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577966} {:answer/id #uuid "7450a216-d7cc-5e17-a4c8-a55b81e7a4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577978} {:answer/id #uuid "bda025e8-26c6-5f35-a552-47e82e40dfe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577993} {:answer/id #uuid "e464f2ee-3ccf-57bb-a644-d46051528921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e464f2ee-3ccf-57bb-a644-d46051528921" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578000} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a17fd82-ba9e-5256-a8c9-61a0ee3e270f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "912af972-d57f-5ca2-b2b1-966a490fc108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x^{6}} + \\frac{e^{3 x}}{3 x^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577985} {:answer/id #uuid "bd5c789c-528d-50bb-880c-295cb5df8150" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x^{5}} + \\frac{e^{3 x}}{3 x^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577992} {:answer/id #uuid "e267e0a0-6b8c-55c3-b57f-4751aed53084" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x^{6}} + \\frac{e^{3 x}}{2 x^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577999} {:answer/id #uuid "f0fc5d89-3460-5b2c-936a-5549c671fa32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x^{5}} + \\frac{e^{3 x}}{2 x^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186578003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "912af972-d57f-5ca2-b2b1-966a490fc108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{C}{x^{6}} + \\frac{e^{3 x}}{3 x^{6}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577985} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b59f443-9037-5baa-accb-c0e1753df732" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186577963}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f95006c4-b3c1-5054-956d-113fccb90ca1" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}y=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{{x}^{6}},\\,x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=\\frac{6}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{{x}^{6}}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (6/x)\\,dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{6})} \\\\  & ={x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x)={x}^{6}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{6}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{6}{x}y) & ={x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{3x}}{{x}^{6}} \\\\ {x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6{x}^{5}\\cdot y & ={e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y\\cdot I(x))$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(y{x}^{6})\\,dx & =\\int {e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{6} & =\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{3}+C}{{x}^{6}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Integrating Factors"}]}
